    {
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 4,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 22
      },
      "id": 8,
      "title": "Known blind spot",
      "type": "text",
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**Coverage here is peer-relative, not absolute.** It's computed as `samples_seen / max-among-node-peers` -- if one rank falls behind its 7 peers, that shows up directly as a fraction below 1.0. But **this is blind to sample loss that hits every rank on a node uniformly** (e.g. a node-wide aggregator issue, or a dedup problem affecting all 8 ranks equally). In that case every rank would still agree with its peers -- all read the same reduced count -- and this panel would show a flat 1.0 across the board even though the *true* expected rate (from the workload's calibrated healthy baseline, ~50/sec/rank for nanoGPT bucket B/C) was higher than what's actually arriving.\n\n**Not fixed here, only documented** -- matching how the Network panel below documents its own gap rather than papering over it. A real fix would need an absolute baseline (the calibrated rate) compared against `rate(agg_samples_seen[...])`, not just cross-rank agreement."
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {},
        "overrides": []
      },
      "transparent": false
    },
